<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carthago delenda est</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carthago delenda est, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> must be destroyed&quot;, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> phrase associated with the Roman senator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cato-the-elder">Cato the Elder</a>, who is said to have ended his speeches with it. It expresses Roman hostility to Carthage in the years before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-punic-war">Third Punic War</a>. The war ended with the destruction of Carthage and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a> in 146 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carthago-delenda-est.jpg" alt="Carthago delenda est" width="271" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ritratto_maschile_detto_il_vecchio_di_otricoli,_50_ac_ca._(busto_moderno),_da_otricoli_(visconti),_MT533,_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> won the first two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a> but suffered humiliations and damaging reverses, above all at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Battle of Cannae</a> in 216 BCE, and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a> in 201 BCE. Carthage was then reduced to a small territory equal to what is now north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and no longer threatened Rome. In 152 BCE Cato the Censor, a veteran of that war, visited the city as a member of a senatorial embassy sent to arbitrate a conflict between Carthage and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Massinissa</a>, king of Numidia and an ally of Rome. He was shocked by Carthage's wealth, which he considered dangerous for Rome.</p><p>Cato then called relentlessly for the city's destruction and is said to have ended all his speeches with the phrase, even when the debate concerned another matter. The Senate did not follow him at first. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-cornelius-scipio-nasica-corculum">Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Corculum</a>, son-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a> and the most influential senator, opposed war, arguing that fear of a common enemy was needed to maintain Roman unity. He is said to have ended his own speeches with Carthago servanda est, '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> must be saved'.</p><p>Cato prevailed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Massinissa</a>, which gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> a pretext, since the treaty of 201 BCE forbade Carthage to declare war without Rome's assent. In 146 BCE Scipio Aemilianus, grandson of Africanus, razed the city and its remaining population was sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> became a Roman province. The notion that Roman forces sowed the city with salt is a 19th-century invention.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>No ancient source gives the phrase exactly as it is quoted today. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> in his Natural History, Aurelius Victor and Florus all quote it in indirect speech, as a demand that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> be destroyed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>'s Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cato-the-elder">Cato the Elder</a> gives only a Greek paraphrase: 'It seems best to me that Carthage no longer exist'. The modern short form was made by English and French scholars at the turn of the 18th and 19th centuries, while German scholars have used the longer Ceterum censeo Carthaginem delendam esse.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The phrase has been quoted or paraphrased as a learned reference to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/total-war">total war</a>. In 1673 the English minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-ashley-cooper-1st-earl-of-shaftesbury">Anthony Ashley Cooper, 1st Earl of Shaftesbury</a>, revived it as Delenda est Carthago in a speech before Parliament during the Third Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-dutch-war">Dutch War</a>, comparing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. In 1899 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Leo Tolstoy</a> kept the form Carthago delenda est as the title of a pacifist essay condemning war and militarism. In 1930 José Ortega y Gasset coined Delenda est Monarchia. The opening words, ceterum censeo, now refer on their own to a statement a speaker repeats again and again.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carthago delenda est” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cato the Elder is said to have ended his speeches with the declaration that Carthage must be destroyed (<b>Carthago delenda est</b>), and the Third Punic War of 149–146 BCE was a siege of a city that posed little real threat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
